gpui_windows: Window control for app-drawn title bars

Apps that draw their own title bar need the window controls that Wayland
and X11 already offer. On Windows, GPUI gains:

- start_window_move, start_window_resize and show_window_menu. They run
  native modal loops, so they start after GPUI's update returns.
- Client decorations (`WindowDecorations::Client`): the whole window is
  drawable, keeps native resize edges, and a maximized window's client is
  clipped to the work area. request_decorations changes them at runtime.
- set_resizable, set_minimizable, set_maximizable and set_maximized at
  runtime, and is_in_live_resize. Maximize permission follows resize
  permission until it is set.
- A native resize constraint (`NativeResizeConstraint`), applied in
  WM_SIZING and WM_GETMINMAXINFO without entering GPUI.
- zoom toggles between maximized and restored; resize measures the frame
  on the UI thread before it applies a client size; a window created
  hidden first shows with the requested activation.

Newport needs it for Remote apps on Windows: Linux apps draw their own
title bars and move, resize and maximize themselves.

The new trait methods have defaults, and other platforms are unchanged.
set_native_resize_constraint exists only on Windows. WindowParams
gains a Windows-only window_decorations field.

Remove when upstream gpui_windows supports client decorations and these
window controls.

